1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
LMs

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Hello and welcome to our course

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Functions, Tools and Agents with Lanchain

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
LMs have demonstrated an amazing ability

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
to interact with humans using natural language

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
which has opened the door to many new applications

7
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
But how can LMs also interact with our existing software infrastructure

8
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
For example, by letting it decide

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
when to make a function call to a different program

10
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
to get more information or to take an action

11
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
LMs were originally designed to generate text for humans

12
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
But some LMs have now been trained to output formatted data

13
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
such as values stored as JSON

14
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
to make it easy to let the LM decide

15
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
when to call other code as a subroutine

16
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
This significantly expands what you can do with LMs

17
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,000
such as let them extract information

18
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,000
from structured or tablet data

19
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
which LMs have typically struggled with

20
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Here to tell you more about this is Harrison Chase

21
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
co-founder and CEO of Lanchain

22
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
who has also talked to two previous short courses

23
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Welcome back Harrison

24
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Thanks Andrew, so great to be back

25
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
You're right, this new capability

26
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:10,000
which OpenAI has named function calling

27
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
is really helpful for developers working with LLMs

28
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Harrison, you've described Lanchain in other courses

29
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
but maybe you could describe what has changed

30
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
and what you'll be covering in this course

31
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Absolutely, as you know Lanchain is an open source library

32
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
that helps developers bridge the gap between traditional software

33
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,000
and LLMs

34
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
It allows developers to support any number of different LLMs

35
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
and provides over 500 integrations to different language models

36
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
vector stores and tools

37
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,000
as well as supporting memory chains and agents

38
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
There are two significant changes that you will be getting into during the course

39
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
the first is the development of Lanchain expression language or LCEL

40
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,000
that makes composing components or chains simpler and more intuitive

41
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
the second is changes to take advantage of the new function calling capability

42
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
you'll learn how to use that directly

43
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
and will also show how it can be used to do tasks like tagging or extracting data

44
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
function calling makes building tools for LLMs simpler and more reliable

45
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
you'll build some tools and then use them to build a conversational agent

46
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Agents can do complex multi-step reasoning

47
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
and can select tools to help them use and solve problems

48
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
you get to use all of those elements in the course

49
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:22,000
and in the final project

50
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
that sounds great Harrison

51
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,000
and I think this will be useful for many people

52
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
that want to learn to take advantage of these new advanced element capabilities

53
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
 many people have worked to make this course possible

54
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
we're grateful to Lance Martin

55
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
and Nuno Kampus from Lanchain

56
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
and on the deep learning.ai side

57
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Jeff Lodwick and Eshmael Gagari also contributed to this course

58
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
so that let's go on to the next video

59
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:49,000
and let's get started

60
